  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1126 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nToday through Saturday night...Southerly flow will increase out \nahead over another front/short wave today, with warmer highs in \nthe mid/upper 60s. Expect warmer overnight lows tonight, ranging\nfrom the mid 40s in the east to the mid 50s in northeast\nLouisiana. /15\n\nSunday - Thursday Night:\n\nAn upper-lvl trough will push a cold front to the region Sunday. \nSoutherly flow will allow for limited moisture/instability to move \ntowards the area, increasing rain/thunderstorm chances. No outlooks \nfor severe weather at this time but strong storm can't be ruled out.\nHigh temperatures will cool to the mid-50s to the lower 60s post \nfrontal boundary on Monday. As a sfc high establish over the \nAppalachian Mountains for the start of the week, temps will undergo \na warming trend through Wednesday. Another Upper/mid-lvl low/trough \nout of the Southwest will push a developing sfc low/cold front \ntowards the Lower MS Valley; providing another chances for showers \nand thunderstorms towards the end of the period (late Thursday into \nFriday). Ahead of the shortwave, south/southwest flow will provide \nadequate instability and moisture return Thursday. Guidance is \nvarying on 1) how much instability/moisture will be available and 2) \narea of concern for any potential severe weather. At this time, \nthere is not an outlook for severe weather from SPC but trends \nwill continued to be monitored as we progress through the forecast\nperiod and check back for updates. /SW/",
